Root cause:
The bug was caused by the inclusion of UNIX-specific code in the native compilation process, which led to compilation errors on Windows platforms after changes made in HADOOP-7824. The code that initializes Java constants related to file permissions was not properly guarded, resulting in attempts to compile it on non-UNIX systems.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
SET_INT_OR_RETURN(env, clazz, S_IXUSR);
```
Reason:
This line attempts to set a Java constant for user execute permissions (`S_IXUSR`), which is a UNIX-specific file permission constant. Since this code is not guarded by a preprocessor directive, it gets compiled on Windows platforms, leading to compilation errors due to the absence of UNIX-specific definitions.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
#ifdef UNIX
SET_INT_OR_RETURN(env, clazz, S_IXUSR);
#endif
```
Reason:
By wrapping the `SET_INT_OR_RETURN` call with `#ifdef UNIX`, the code ensures that this line is only compiled on UNIX systems. This prevents the compilation errors on Windows platforms, as the UNIX-specific code will be excluded from the compilation process on non-UNIX systems.


